    thinking: "**Analyzing Fuel Options**\n\nI\'m currently focused on incorporating an option to add an arbitrary number of fuel types. My prior state variables currently only handle diesel. I\'m considering how to implement this flexibly, likely using an array or object to manage the new fuel information and quantities.\n\n\n**Refining Fuel Data Structure**\n\nI\'ve decided to move forward with a `fuels` state variable as an array of `FuelEntry` objects within `TripForm`. After reviewing `types.ts`, I\'m adding `fuels?: FuelEntry[]` to the `TripEntry` interface. I\'m structuring each fuel entry with properties like `id`, `liters`, `rate`, `amount`, `date`, and optional vendor, payment mode, and status fields. Now, I\'m adapting the `getTripMetrics` function to handle fuel data, summing `liters` and `amount` from the `fuels` array if it exists to update `fuelLiters` and `dieselExpense`.\n\n\n**Adjusting Metric Calculations**\n\nI\'m now revising the `getTripMetrics` function in `types.ts` to accommodate the `fuels` array. I\'m focusing on lines 130 and 134. My thought is that I need to sum the liters and amount from the fuels list if it exists, otherwise use the existing `dieselLiters` and `dieselAmount` logic. I\'m implementing a `hasFuelsList` check and using `reduce` to sum the fuel data. This allows for a smooth transition from the older diesel-only state to the new multi-fuel state, so it should be backward-compatible.\n\n\n**Implementing Fuel Entry Handling**\n\nI\'m incredibly pleased with the integration and metric adaptation. Now I\'m focused on the React state in `TripForm.tsx`, initializing the `fuels` state as an array using `useState`. I\'m using `useEffect` to ensure seamless loading from `editingEntry.fuels || []` and handle the transition from the older diesel-only entries, incorporating a conditional conversion if diesel data exists. The goal is to provide a smooth user experience.\n\n\n**Designing Fuel Entry UI**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the UI design within `TripForm.tsx`, particularly the implementation of adding and removing multiple fuels. I\'ve successfully managed to initialize the `fuels` array with the `useState` hook. Now, I am focused on the layout for adding a new fuel entry. I am thinking of using inputs for fuel liters, rate, date, vendor, and payment mode. I am excited about the smooth transitions.\n\n\n**Developing Fuel Entry Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing new UI elements within `TripForm.tsx`. I\'ve added state variables for `newFuelVendor` and `newFuelPaymentMode`. I\'m deciding between text inputs or option selects for the account selection, and I\'ve chosen option selects. I\'m excited about the added flexibility. Then I plan to display the added fuels in a table or card list. I am also implementing a `useEffect` hook to automatically update `dieselLiters` and `dieselAmount` from the fuels list on state change, which should improve consistency.\n\n\n**Calculating Automatic Legacy Values**\n\nI\'ve just implemented a smart solution to ensure legacy fields like `dieselLiters` and `dieselAmount` remain accurate and compatible. The code efficiently sums the fuel data from the `fuels` array and updates these legacy values automatically, even for existing exports. Now, I\'m shifting focus to calculating driver wages based on a percentage.\n\n\n**Adding Driver Wage Selector**\n\nI\'m now integrating an interactive selector next to the \"Driver Wages / Allowance\" input. My plan is to add a percentage selection, from 1 to 20, to calculate wages. On selection, I\'ll compute and set `stDriverWages` automatically using `Math.round(stIncome * (pct / 100))`. The computed amount will seamlessly update on the form.\n\n\n**Integrating Wage Selector**\n\nI\'m currently focused on integrating the driver wage percentage selector. I am thinking of including it inline with the \"Driver Wages / Allowance\" input. I will add a percentage selection, from 1 to 20, to determine wages automatically, updating the form.\n\n\n**Implementing Wage Logic**\n\nI\'ve just added the driver wage percentage selector and calculations. When a percentage is selected, I\'m updating `stDriverWages` automatically based on `stIncome`. I am using `Math.round` and formatting to provide seamless updates to the form, along with some added visual helper text.\n\n\n**Designing Expense Form**\n\nI\'m now creating a new expense form with fields for Truck No, Expense Type, Shop, Expense Amount, Payment Mode, Date, and Status. I\'m leveraging the provided reference maps for Expense Type, Shop Name, and Payment Mode for dropdown selections, ensuring accurate data entry. I\'m focusing on structuring the `ExpenseMaster` component, envisioning a well-organized layout for capturing and managing expense data.\n\n\n"
